A||N||9||2xsess||Tired of bleeding the &$#@*&^$#@ clutch already||||1||
Z||000000||2xsess||11-04-2000||04:27 PM||||Ok, I've read the 5 page book on clutch problems like twice. Here's my problem. My 85GT 4spd was giving me a bit of shifting trouble so I got a steel clutch pedal and changed that, wow what a difference that made. NOW my damn clutch system is taking in air somewhere and before I go spending around $200 to replace the master and slave cylinders I wanted to see if anyone has done the rebuild kits on either of these.<P>I noticed too that my slave is missing the bellows which makes me think it's the slave causing this trouble. I bled it yesterday and after 10 minutes of driving there's like no pressure, but if I pump it a few times the pressure comes back. And then there's galore in the system. <BR>I called around and Advanced Auto Parts has a slave for $69 in stock. then I looked in the Fiero store catalog and they have the rebuild kits for both for less than that.<P><B>PLEASE HELP</B><P>TIA<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||11-04-2000||04:54 PM||||Keep in mind this:<BR>Air getting in, fluid getting out!<BR>Check the underside and see if there is any rust on the line at attaching points. I just had transmission line problems myself, worth a look.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fieroboy1||11-04-2000||05:13 PM||||My 86 Se had lots of shifting problems mostly stemming from the clutch pedal which you already addressed, the rebuild on the slave is difficult and not 100% guaranteed check all the connections and make sure you have no leaks, but just bite the bullet and change the slave when i changed mine it made a huge difference||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||11-04-2000||05:20 PM||||Like it was stated bite the bullet but go all the way and get a GM master and/or slave(cheaper in the long run).||||reg||1
Z||000004||William||11-04-2000||06:23 PM||||I was having this problem too. There was a big discussion on this topic last year. The consensus was that the aftermarket slaves would suck air when bleeding the system. Not much was said about the master so I got that from the local store(New Raybestos). The slave was obtained from the dealer as the stories were worring me. The clutch has been fine ever since. Order soon as I had to wait a week for the dealer to get one from the west coast. That was 2 weeks ago.||||reg||1
Z||000005||2M4 Dale||11-04-2000||06:53 PM||||I have a new Raybestos on mine and it works just fine !!!    Buy a new one Jeff !!!||||reg||1
Z||000006||2xsess||11-04-2000||08:24 PM||||Ok, So I'll get the new slave and see if that's the problem, I'm going to see what brand Advanced Auto Parts has tomorrow if it's an off the wall brand I'll pass and order one from GM.<P>Oh and Indiana Resto Guy, I'm thinking that if it was leaking at the slave I wouldn't really notice because of bleeding it and having the fluid all over the place anyways.<P>Thanks for all your help, I'll probably post progress here so hopefully anyone else with similar problems can learn along with me.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 2xsess (edited 11-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||11-04-2000||08:42 PM||||The AutoZone slave cylinders are cast iron.  I really would suggest buying one from the dealer.  They are a little more, but very nice and worth the price!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bob Englert||11-04-2000||10:20 PM||||After many frustrations, I personally will never use anything but the genuine GM clutch hydralics in my cars.<P>Learned my lesson!<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000009||2xsess||11-05-2000||05:15 PM||||Weel, I went and got the one from Advanced auto, I do't really have a choice since I have to have a car on Monday, it was a crapy cast iron thing, I took the old one apart hoping to just replace the old with the stuff out of the new one but the inside of the old cylinder is rusty so I just put the whole new one in and so far it seems to have solved my problem.<P>Thanks for all your help. I think I might just order the rebuild kit and very carefully get the rust out of the old one and rebuild it for the next time.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/My_car_(large)sig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
